--- A literal list of ints at the top level, then a float written at one of its
--- positions (`ys[0] = 2.5`), is an internal error in both modes.
---
--- crow run -i and crow run alike (exit 1, nothing printed):
---     gotchas-1.co: internal error: the lowered program disagrees with itself — a
---     bug in Crow, not in this program: gotchas-1 main: line 19: an item of 'ys',
---     a list of Int, is assigned Float
--- Expected (both modes; spec, Indexing: "a list or a map of ints written a float
--- holds floats from then on, as a variable does"):
---     [2.5, 2]
---
--- Runs in both modes when: the same three lines are in a function's body; the
--- list is made by `range[2]` or a collecting loop; the float is pushed
--- (`ys = push[ys, 2.5]`); an int is written (`ys[0] = 3`). Fails the same with
--- a computed position (`ys[i] = 2.5`), a float held in a local (`x = 2.5`,
--- `ys[0] = x`), and with a read of the list before the write. Found writing the
--- Gotchas task (to show that a list of ints given one float is a list of floats).
ys = [1, 2]
ys[0] = 2.5
log[ys]
